                <head>162. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>Under Secretary of State Douglas Dillon visited Yugoslavia July 17–20, during a
                    five-nation good will tour. On July 18 at 9:30 a.m., Dillon met with Yugoslav
                    Vice President Todorovic (see <ref target="#d163">Document 163</ref>). After
                    lunch with Todorovic, Dillon visited the Pancevo fertilizer plant. That evening
                    he attended a dinner at the U.S. Embassy. At 11 a.m. on July 19, Dillon met with
                    President Tito at the latter’s villa on the island of Vanga (see <ref
                        target="#d164">Documents 164</ref>–<ref target="#d168">168</ref>). After his
                    discussions with Tito, Dillon returned to the Yugoslav mainland and drove to
                    Zagreb. Dillon flew from Zagreb to Paris the morning of July 20. Documentation
                    on Dillon’s visit to Yugoslavia, including memoranda of conversation, summary
                    telegrams, and briefing papers, is in Department of State, Conference Files: Lot
                    64 D 559, CF 1724.</p>
            </div>
